\title{\bfseries Composing Locality with Hiding:\\ An Exact Factorization of Perfect Zero Knowledge\\ for Committed Local-Oracle Protocols}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{26 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We isolate, in a finite combinatorial setting, the exact mechanism by which the standard ``probabilistically checkable proof plus commitment'' compiler yields zero knowledge. We introduce the notion of a \emph{committed local-oracle protocol}: a randomized proof string $\Prf : P \to (I \to A)$ over a coordinate set $I$ and alphabet $A$, a commitment scheme $(\Com,\Open)$ with randomness space $R_c$, and an honest verifier who, on coins $r \in R_v$, opens a query set $Q(r) \subseteq I$ of size at most a constant $q$. The transcript of an execution is the quadruple (commitment, verifier coins, opened partial assignment, opening data).

Our main theorem states that two \emph{independent} hypotheses compose exactly. The first, \emph{perfect hiding of unopened coordinates}, requires that any two messages agreeing on a set $T$ be carried into one another by a bijection of the commitment randomness preserving both the commitment and the openings on $T$. The second, \emph{perfect simulation of the opened coordinates}, requires that for each fixing of the verifier's coins the distribution of the opened partial assignment be reproduced exactly by a witness-free simulator. Under these hypotheses the \emph{full} transcript distribution of the real interaction coincides with the simulator's, transcript by transcript: the protocol is \emph{perfect honest-verifier zero knowledge}. Consequently every event has equal probability under the two distributions and every computationally unbounded distinguisher has advantage exactly zero.

The proof is a fibrewise counting argument over the restriction map $u \mapsto u|_{Q(r)}$. We supply a proved instance of hiding --- the coordinate-wise one-time pad over an arbitrary finite abelian group --- and an end-to-end instance of the full theorem: the committed $2$-query probabilistically checkable proof for graph $3$-colourability is perfect honest-verifier zero knowledge, the simulation resource being exactly the \emph{sharp $2$-transitivity} of the symmetric group on three letters. Finally we delimit the theorem: each hypothesis is necessary, and mere transitivity of the rerandomization group is provably insufficient --- cyclic recolouring admits \textbf{no} simulator over any randomness space.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} zero knowledge, probabilistically checkable proofs, commitment schemes, query complexity, sharp $2$-transitivity, graph $3$-colouring, perfect simulation.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The problem}

A zero-knowledge proof lets a prover convince a verifier of a statement while transferring no information beyond the statement's truth. The canonical construction for $\mathsf{NP}$ combines two ingredients: a \emph{local verifier}, reading only a constant number $q$ of symbols of a possibly enormous proof string; and a \emph{commitment scheme}, allowing the prover to fix the proof string in advance and later reveal selected symbols.

Soundness of the resulting protocol is a statement about the local verifier alone and is not our subject. Zero knowledge is subtler: the verifier genuinely receives data --- $q$ symbols per round, plus a commitment and the corresponding openings --- and the claim that this data is ``nothing'' is a claim about \emph{distributions}, formalized by requiring that a witness-free simulator reproduce the transcript distribution.

Textbook treatments interleave three distinct concerns: the algebra of the commitment, the symmetry of the alphabet, and the smallness of the query set. Our purpose is to \emph{separate} them, and to show that the separation is exact rather than approximate.

\subsection{Contributions}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{A definition:} the \emph{committed local-oracle protocol} (Section~\ref{sec:model}), packaging commitment, opening, query pattern, query bound and randomized proof string, with an explicit transcript space in which unopened coordinates are formally absent.
\item \textbf{Two resources:} perfect hiding of unopened coordinates (Definition~\ref{def:hiding}) and perfect simulation of the opened coordinates (Definition~\ref{def:sim}), stated as finite counting/bijection statements requiring no measure theory.
\item \textbf{An exact composition theorem} (Theorem~\ref{thm:main}), with corollaries of event-wise equality and zero distinguishing advantage.
\item \textbf{A proved hiding instance} (Theorem~\ref{thm:otp}): the coordinate-wise one-time pad over any finite abelian group.
\item \textbf{An end-to-end instance} (Theorem~\ref{thm:3col}): the committed $2$-query proof for graph $3$-colourability, with the zero-knowledge content identified as sharp $2$-transitivity of $\Sym(\mathbb{Z}/3)$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp}).
\item \textbf{Tightness} (Section~\ref{sec:tight}): each hypothesis is necessary at query complexity one, and mere transitivity of the alphabet symmetry group is insufficient (Theorem~\ref{thm:cyclic}).
\end{enumerate}

\section{The model}\label{sec:model}

All index sets, alphabets, message spaces and randomness spaces are finite, and all distributions are uniform on their randomness spaces. Fix finite types: $I$ (coordinates), $A$ (alphabet), $C$ (commitment messages), $O$ (opening data), $R_c$ (commitment randomness), $R_v$ (verifier randomness), $P$ (prover randomness), $S$ (simulator randomness).

\subsection{Partial views}

\begin{definition}[Restriction]
For $T \subseteq I$ and $f : I \to A$, the \emph{restriction of $f$ to $T$} is the partial assignment
\[
f|_T : I \to A \cup \{\bot\}, \qquad f|_T(i) = \begin{cases} f(i) & i \in T,\\ \bot & i \notin T.\end{cases}
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Restrictions agree iff functions agree on $T$]\label{lem:restr}
$f|_T = g|_T$ if and only if $f(i) = g(i)$ for all $i \in T$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
If the restrictions are equal, evaluating at $i \in T$ gives $f(i)=g(i)$. Conversely define both sides pointwise and split on $i \in T$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Only opened coordinates are defined]\label{lem:isSome}
If $f|_T(i) \neq \bot$ then $i \in T$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Immediate from the definition.
\end{proof}

Lemma~\ref{lem:isSome} makes the transcript space \emph{honest}: unopened coordinates leave no trace in the recorded partial assignment.

\subsection{Committed local-oracle protocols}

\begin{definition}[Committed local-oracle protocol]\label{def:protocol}
A \emph{committed local-oracle protocol} over $(I,A,C,O,R_c,R_v,P)$ consists of a commitment map $\Com : (I \to A)\times R_c \to C$; an opening map $\Open : (I\to A)\times R_c \times \mathcal{P}(I) \to O$; a query pattern $Q : R_v \to \mathcal{P}(I)$; a query bound $q \in \mathbb{N}$ with $|Q(r)|\le q$ for all $r$; and a randomized proof string $\Prf : P \to (I \to A)$.
\end{definition}

Semantics: the prover samples $p \in P$, $\rho \in R_c$ uniformly and publishes $\Com(\Prf(p),\rho)$; the verifier samples $r \in R_v$ uniformly and announces $Q(r)$; the prover reveals the symbols of $\Prf(p)$ on $Q(r)$ together with $\Open(\Prf(p),\rho,Q(r))$.

\begin{definition}[Transcript]
The \emph{transcript space} is $\mathcal{T} = C \times R_v \times (I \to A\cup\{\bot\}) \times O$. The \emph{real transcript} on $(p,\rho,r)$ is
\[
\Real(p,\rho,r) = \bigl(\Com(\Prf(p),\rho),\ r,\ \Prf(p)|_{Q(r)},\ \Open(\Prf(p),\rho,Q(r))\bigr),
\]
and, for a simulator $\Sm : R_v \times S \to (I \to A)$, the \emph{simulated transcript} on $(s,\rho,r)$ is
\[
\Simu(s,\rho,r) = \bigl(\Com(\Sm(r,s),\rho),\ r,\ \Sm(r,s)|_{Q(r)},\ \Open(\Sm(r,s),\rho,Q(r))\bigr).
\]
\end{definition}

The simulator commits and opens \emph{honestly}; its only freedom is which string to commit to.

\begin{proposition}[Constant-query locality of the transcript]\label{prop:local}
For every $p \in P$ and $r \in R_v$,
\[
\bigl|\{\, i \in I : \Prf(p)|_{Q(r)}(i) \neq \bot \,\}\bigr| \le q .
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:isSome} the set is contained in $Q(r)$, of cardinality at most $q$.
\end{proof}

\section{The two resources}

\subsection{Hiding}

\begin{definition}[Fibre count]
For $u : I \to A$, $T \subseteq I$, $c\in C$, $o \in O$,
\[
\fib(u,T,c,o) = \bigl|\{\, \rho \in R_c : \Com(u,\rho) = c \text{ and } \Open(u,\rho,T) = o \,\}\bigr| .
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Perfect hiding of unopened coordinates]\label{def:hiding}
A committed local-oracle protocol \emph{perfectly hides unopened coordinates} if for every $T \subseteq I$ and all $u,v : I \to A$ with $u(i)=v(i)$ for all $i \in T$, there is a bijection $e : R_c \to R_c$ such that for all $\rho \in R_c$,
\[
\Com(u,\rho) = \Com(v,e(\rho)) \qquad\text{and}\qquad \Open(u,\rho,T) = \Open(v,e(\rho),T).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{remark}
The condition is a \emph{re-labelling principle}: messages indistinguishable on the opened set are indistinguishable in the transcript, uniformly in the commitment randomness. One could instead demand equality of pushforward measures on $C\times O$; for finite randomness spaces the formulations are equivalent up to Lemma~\ref{lem:fibcongr}, and the bijection form is what concrete schemes actually supply.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Simulation}

\begin{definition}[Local view distributions]
For $r \in R_v$ and a partial assignment $t$,
\[
\Nreal(r,t) = \bigl|\{ p \in P : \Prf(p)|_{Q(r)} = t \}\bigr|,\qquad
\Nsim(r,t) = \bigl|\{ s \in S : \Sm(r,s)|_{Q(r)} = t \}\bigr| .
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Perfect simulation of the opened coordinates]\label{def:sim}
A simulator $\Sm$ \emph{perfectly simulates the opened coordinates} if for all $r \in R_v$ and all $t$,
\[
\Nreal(r,t)\cdot |S| \;=\; \Nsim(r,t)\cdot|P| .
\]
\end{definition}

Dividing, this says $\Nreal(r,t)/|P| = \Nsim(r,t)/|S|$. The cross-multiplied form is an identity of natural numbers; the rational statement follows at the end in one step.

\begin{theorem}[Bijection criterion]\label{thm:bij}
Suppose for each $r \in R_v$ there is a bijection $\Phi_r : P \to S$ with
$\Prf(p)|_{Q(r)} = \Sm(r,\Phi_r(p))|_{Q(r)}$ for all $p \in P$. Then $\Sm$ perfectly simulates the opened coordinates.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Fix $r$. Since $\Phi_r$ is a bijection, $|P|=|S|$. Moreover $\Phi_r$ restricts to a bijection between $\{p : \Prf(p)|_{Q(r)} = t\}$ and $\{s : \Sm(r,s)|_{Q(r)} = t\}$: it maps the former into the latter by view preservation, it is injective, and it is surjective onto the latter since any such $s$ has a preimage whose view equals that of $s$. Hence $\Nreal(r,t)=\Nsim(r,t)$; multiply by the equal cardinalities.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:bij} is the abstract form of ``the prover's rerandomization group acts sharply transitively on the admissible local views''.

\section{Counting machinery}

\begin{definition}[Transcript counts and probabilities]
For $\tau \in \mathcal{T}$,
\[
\RC(\tau) = \bigl|\{(p,\rho,r) : \Real(p,\rho,r)=\tau\}\bigr|,\qquad
\SC(\tau) = \bigl|\{(s,\rho,r) : \Simu(s,\rho,r)=\tau\}\bigr|,
\]
\[
\Pr\nolimits_{\mathrm{real}}(\tau) = \frac{\RC(\tau)}{|P|\,|R_c|\,|R_v|},\qquad
\Pr\nolimits_{\mathrm{sim}}(\tau) = \frac{\SC(\tau)}{|S|\,|R_c|\,|R_v|},
\]
with the convention $x/0 = 0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Hiding makes the fibre count depend only on the opened part]\label{lem:fibcongr}
Assume perfect hiding of unopened coordinates. If $u(i)=v(i)$ for all $i \in T$ then $\fib(u,T,c,o) = \fib(v,T,c,o)$ for all $c,o$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Let $e$ be the bijection of Definition~\ref{def:hiding} for $(T,u,v)$. If $\Com(u,\rho)=c$ and $\Open(u,\rho,T)=o$, then $\Com(v,e(\rho))=c$ and $\Open(v,e(\rho),T)=o$, so $e$ maps the first fibre into the second; applying the defining identities at $\rho = e^{-1}(\sigma)$ shows $e^{-1}$ maps the second into the first. The two maps are mutually inverse.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Fibrewise decomposition of the real count]\label{lem:realsum}
For all $c,r_0,t,o$,
\[
\RC(c,r_0,t,o) = \sum_{p \in P} \bigl[\Prf(p)|_{Q(r_0)} = t\bigr]\cdot \fib\bigl(\Prf(p),Q(r_0),c,o\bigr).
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Expand $\RC$ as a triple sum of indicators. The transcript records $r$ verbatim, so for fixed $(p,\rho)$ the sum over $r$ collapses to $r=r_0$; the surviving indicator is that of the conjunction $\Com(\Prf(p),\rho)=c$, $\Prf(p)|_{Q(r_0)}=t$, $\Open(\Prf(p),\rho,Q(r_0))=o$. The middle conjunct is independent of $\rho$ and factors out; the remaining sum over $\rho$ is the fibre count.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Fibrewise decomposition of the simulated count]\label{lem:simsum}
Identically,
\[
\SC(c,r_0,t,o) = \sum_{s \in S} \bigl[\Sm(r_0,s)|_{Q(r_0)} = t\bigr]\cdot \fib\bigl(\Sm(r_0,s),Q(r_0),c,o\bigr).
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Verbatim the proof of Lemma~\ref{lem:realsum} with $\Prf(p)$ replaced by $\Sm(r_0,s)$.
\end{proof}

Crucially, the right-hand sides of Lemmas~\ref{lem:realsum} and \ref{lem:simsum} never mention any coordinate outside $Q(r_0)$: the count factors into a \emph{horizontal} term (which randomness produces the visible view $t$) and a \emph{vertical} term (how many commitment coins are consistent with $(c,o)$).

\section{The composition theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Exact composition; perfect honest-verifier zero knowledge]\label{thm:main}
Let a committed local-oracle protocol perfectly hide unopened coordinates, and let $\Sm$ perfectly simulate the opened coordinates. Assume $P$ and $S$ are non-empty. Then for every $\tau \in \mathcal{T}$,
\[
\Pr\nolimits_{\mathrm{real}}(\tau) = \Pr\nolimits_{\mathrm{sim}}(\tau).
\]
Moreover, assuming only $P \neq \emptyset$ and with no assumption on $R_c$ or $R_v$, the integral identity $\RC(\tau)\cdot|S| = \SC(\tau)\cdot|P|$ holds.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $\tau = (c,r_0,t,o)$ and $T = Q(r_0)$.

\textbf{Step 1 (Decompose).} By Lemmas~\ref{lem:realsum} and~\ref{lem:simsum},
\[
\RC(\tau) = \sum_{p \in F_{\mathrm{real}}}\fib(\Prf(p),T,c,o),\qquad
\SC(\tau) = \sum_{s \in F_{\mathrm{sim}}}\fib(\Sm(r_0,s),T,c,o),
\]
where $F_{\mathrm{real}} = \{p : \Prf(p)|_T = t\}$ and $F_{\mathrm{sim}} = \{s : \Sm(r_0,s)|_T = t\}$, so $|F_{\mathrm{real}}| = \Nreal(r_0,t)$ and $|F_{\mathrm{sim}}| = \Nsim(r_0,t)$.

\textbf{Step 2 (Empty fibre).} If $F_{\mathrm{real}} = \emptyset$ then $\Nreal(r_0,t)=0$, so Definition~\ref{def:sim} gives $\Nsim(r_0,t)\cdot|P| = 0$; as $|P|>0$ we get $\Nsim(r_0,t)=0$ and $F_{\mathrm{sim}}=\emptyset$. Both counts vanish and the identity is trivial. (This is the only use of $P \neq \emptyset$.)

\textbf{Step 3 (Hiding flattens the summand).} Otherwise choose $p_0 \in F_{\mathrm{real}}$ and set $N := \fib(\Prf(p_0),T,c,o)$. For $p \in F_{\mathrm{real}}$ we have $\Prf(p)|_T = t = \Prf(p_0)|_T$, hence by Lemma~\ref{lem:restr} the strings agree pointwise on $T$, hence by Lemma~\ref{lem:fibcongr} their fibre counts equal $N$. The same argument applies to $s \in F_{\mathrm{sim}}$, since $\Sm(r_0,s)|_T = t = \Prf(p_0)|_T$; Lemma~\ref{lem:fibcongr} is indifferent to the provenance of the message, so the \emph{same} constant $N$ governs both sides. Therefore
\[
\RC(\tau) = \Nreal(r_0,t)\cdot N,\qquad \SC(\tau) = \Nsim(r_0,t)\cdot N .
\]

\textbf{Step 4 (Simulation matches fibre sizes).}
\[
\RC(\tau)\cdot|S| = \bigl(\Nreal(r_0,t)\cdot|S|\bigr)N = \bigl(\Nsim(r_0,t)\cdot|P|\bigr)N = \SC(\tau)\cdot|P| .
\]

\textbf{Step 5 (Divide).} If $|R_c| = 0$ or $|R_v| = 0$, both probabilities are $0$ by the convention $x/0=0$. Otherwise all four cardinalities are positive and cross-multiplication of Step~4 by $|R_c||R_v|$ gives exactly $\Pr_{\mathrm{real}}(\tau) = \Pr_{\mathrm{sim}}(\tau)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[No event distinguishes]
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:main}, for every finite $E \subseteq \mathcal{T}$,
$\sum_{\tau\in E}\Pr_{\mathrm{real}}(\tau) = \sum_{\tau\in E}\Pr_{\mathrm{sim}}(\tau)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Zero distinguishing advantage]
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:main}, for every finite $E \subseteq \mathcal{T}$ and every scoring function $f : \mathcal{T}\to\mathbb{Q}$, with no restriction on the resources used to compute $f$,
\[
\sum_{\tau\in E} f(\tau)\Pr\nolimits_{\mathrm{real}}(\tau) \;-\; \sum_{\tau\in E} f(\tau)\Pr\nolimits_{\mathrm{sim}}(\tau) \;=\; 0 .
\]
\end{corollary}

The advantage is exactly zero, not negligible; there is no security parameter and no asymptotics anywhere in the argument.

\subsection{What the proof reveals}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lll@{}}
\toprule
Direction & Controls & Mechanism \\
\midrule
Vertical ($R_c$) & unopened coordinates & hiding: bijection of commitment randomness, flattening $\fib$ to $N$ \\
Horizontal ($P$ vs.\ $S$) & opened coordinates & simulation: equality of fibre cardinalities \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Locality enters not as a hypothesis of Theorem~\ref{thm:main} but as the reason the horizontal direction is tractable: the fibre of the restriction map is determined by at most $q$ symbols, so a witness-free simulator need only reproduce a $q$-dimensional marginal. If $q = |I|$, reproducing the marginal is reproducing the entire proof distribution.

\section{A proved hiding instance: the one-time pad}

Let $A$ be a finite abelian group. Define a protocol with $R_c = (I \to A)$ by
\[
\Com(u,\rho) = u+\rho \ \text{(coordinate-wise)},\qquad \Open(u,\rho,T) = \rho|_T ,
\]
with any query pattern satisfying $|Q(r)|\le q$ and any randomized proof string. Openings are informative: from $u+\rho$ and $\rho|_T$ the verifier recovers $u|_T$. It is precisely because openings are informative that hiding must be established \emph{jointly} for the commitment and the openings.

\begin{theorem}[The one-time pad perfectly hides unopened coordinates]\label{thm:otp}
The protocol above perfectly hides unopened coordinates.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $u,v$ agree on $T$ and take $e(\rho) = \rho + (u-v)$, translation by a fixed element of the group $I\to A$, hence a bijection. For the commitment, for all $i$,
\[
\bigl(v+e(\rho)\bigr)(i) = v(i)+\rho(i)+u(i)-v(i) = u(i)+\rho(i) = (u+\rho)(i).
\]
For the openings, if $i \in T$ then $u(i)=v(i)$, so $e(\rho)(i) = \rho(i)$; hence $e(\rho)|_T = \rho|_T$. Off $T$ both restrictions are $\bot$.
\end{proof}

Combining Theorems~\ref{thm:otp} and~\ref{thm:main} reduces perfect honest-verifier zero knowledge of \emph{any} one-time-padded local-oracle protocol to a single question about the alphabet: is the $q$-symbol local view witness-independent?

\section{End-to-end instance: the committed $2$-query proof for graph $3$-colouring}

\subsection{The protocol}

Let $V$ be a finite vertex set and $E \subseteq V\times V$ a finite edge set; the alphabet is $A = \mathbb{Z}/3$.

\begin{definition}
A colouring $c : V \to \mathbb{Z}/3$ is \emph{proper} for $E$ if $c(e_1)\neq c(e_2)$ for every $e = (e_1,e_2)\in E$.
\end{definition}

The protocol $\mathcal{P}_{E,c}$ has coordinates $I = V$; prover randomness $P = \Sym(\mathbb{Z}/3)$ with proof string $\Prf(\pi) = \pi\circ c$; the coordinate-wise one-time pad as commitment, $R_c = (V\to\mathbb{Z}/3)$; verifier randomness $R_v = E$ with query pattern $Q(e) = \{e_1,e_2\}$, whence $q = 2$. By Proposition~\ref{prop:local}, a transcript exposes at most two symbols of the proof string.

\subsection{The simulation resource: sharp $2$-transitivity}

\begin{theorem}[Sharp $2$-transitivity of $\Sym(\mathbb{Z}/3)$]\label{thm:sharp}
Let $x\neq y$ and $a \neq b$ in $\mathbb{Z}/3$. Then there is \emph{exactly one} permutation $\pi$ of $\mathbb{Z}/3$ with $\pi(x)=a$ and $\pi(y)=b$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
\emph{Existence.} Let $\pi_1$ be the transposition swapping $x$ and $a$ (the identity if $x=a$), so $\pi_1(x)=a$; injectivity and $y\neq x$ give $\pi_1(y)\neq a$. Let $\pi_2$ swap $\pi_1(y)$ and $b$, and set $\pi = \pi_2\pi_1$. Then $\pi(y)=b$, and $\pi(x) = \pi_2(a) = a$ since $a \notin \{\pi_1(y), b\}$.

\emph{Uniqueness.} Suppose $\pi,\sigma$ both send $x\mapsto a$, $y\mapsto b$, and let $z \in \mathbb{Z}/3$. If $z\in\{x,y\}$ then $\pi(z)=\sigma(z)$. Otherwise injectivity gives $\pi(z),\sigma(z)\notin\{a,b\}$; in a three-element set exactly one element is distinct from the two distinct elements $a,b$, so $\pi(z)=\sigma(z)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The opened pair is uniform]\label{cor:uniform}
Let $c$ be proper for $E$ and $e \in E$. For any ordered pair $(a,b)$ of distinct colours,
\[
\bigl|\{\pi \in \Sym(\mathbb{Z}/3) : \pi(c(e_1))=a,\ \pi(c(e_2))=b\}\bigr| = 1 .
\]
Hence the pair of colours opened on the challenged edge is exactly uniform over the six ordered pairs of distinct colours, independently of $c$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Properness gives $c(e_1)\neq c(e_2)$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp}. Six permutations and six ordered distinct pairs, each hit once.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The simulator and the theorem}

Let $S = \{(a,b)\in(\mathbb{Z}/3)^2 : a\neq b\}$, of cardinality $6$, and define
\[
\Sm(e,(a,b))(w) = \begin{cases} a & w = e_1,\\ b & w = e_2,\\ 0 & \text{otherwise.}\end{cases}
\]
The simulator never inspects $c$.

\begin{theorem}[Perfect honest-verifier zero knowledge of the committed $2$-query proof for $3$-colouring]\label{thm:3col}
Let $c$ be proper for $E$. Then for every transcript $\tau$, $\Pr_{\mathrm{real}}(\tau) = \Pr_{\mathrm{sim}}(\tau)$, where the real distribution is induced by uniform palette permutation, uniform pad and uniform edge, and the simulated one by uniform distinct colour pair, uniform pad and uniform edge.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
\emph{Hiding.} The commitment is the coordinate-wise one-time pad over $\mathbb{Z}/3$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:otp}.

\emph{Simulation.} Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:bij} with $\Phi_e(\pi) = \bigl(\pi(c(e_1)),\pi(c(e_2))\bigr)$. This is well defined (properness and injectivity give distinct entries), injective (two permutations agreeing at the two distinct points $c(e_1),c(e_2)$ coincide, by the uniqueness half of Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp}), and surjective (existence half). It preserves the opened view: on $Q(e)=\{e_1,e_2\}$ we have $\Prf(\pi)(e_1) = \pi(c(e_1)) = \Sm(e,\Phi_e(\pi))(e_1)$ and likewise at $e_2$, using $e_1\neq e_2$ (a consequence of properness); apply Lemma~\ref{lem:restr}.

Both randomness spaces have six elements, so Theorem~\ref{thm:main} applies.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Perfect completeness]
If $c$ is proper for $E$ then $\pi(c(e_1))\neq\pi(c(e_2))$ for every permutation $\pi$ and edge $e$, so the honest verifier --- who accepts iff the two revealed colours differ --- accepts with probability $1$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Properness gives $c(e_1)\neq c(e_2)$; injectivity of $\pi$ preserves this.
\end{proof}

Properness is load-bearing in Theorem~\ref{thm:3col}: for an improper colouring the two opened symbols could coincide on a violated edge, whereas the simulator never outputs equal colours. Self-loops are automatically excluded by properness.

\section{Tightness: each hypothesis is necessary}\label{sec:tight}

\subsection{Hiding is necessary}

\begin{construction}[Leaking protocol]
Take $I = \{0,1\}$, $A = \mathbb{Z}/2$, and one-element $R_c$, $R_v$, $P$. Let the proof string be $0$ at coordinate $0$ and $1$ at coordinate $1$; let $Q(r)=\{0\}$ (so $q=1$); and let the ``commitment'' be the identity $\Com(u,\rho)=u$, with trivial opening data.
\end{construction}

\begin{proposition}
For this protocol: (i) the opened coordinate is perfectly simulated by the simulator writing $0$ everywhere; (ii) unopened coordinates are \emph{not} hidden --- the proof string and the all-zero string agree on $\{0\}$, yet their commitments (the strings themselves) differ at coordinate $1$, so no bijection can equate them; (iii) perfect honest-verifier zero knowledge fails maximally: the honest transcript has probability $1$ in the real interaction and $0$ in the simulation.
\end{proposition}

Items (i) and (iii) together are the instructive point: the leak is \emph{invisible in the opened view}, so simulation of the local view says nothing about what the commitment may be spilling.

\subsection{Simulation is necessary}

\begin{construction}[Padded protocol with a wrong simulator]
Take $I = \{0\}$, $A = \mathbb{Z}/2$, the one-time-pad commitment (so $|R_c| = 2$), proof string constantly $0$, $Q(r)=\{0\}$, and the simulator outputting the constant string $1$.
\end{construction}

\begin{proposition}
Hiding holds by Theorem~\ref{thm:otp}, but simulation fails --- the real opened view is $0\mapsto 0$ with probability $1$, the simulated view $0\mapsto1$ with probability $1$ --- and perfect honest-verifier zero knowledge fails: the transcript arising from the zero pad has probability $1/2$ in the real interaction and $0$ in the simulation.
\end{proposition}

Both failures occur at query complexity one, the least possible for a non-trivial protocol.

\subsection{Transitivity is not enough}

\begin{theorem}[No simulator for shift rerandomization]\label{thm:cyclic}
Consider the two-vertex, one-edge instance $V=\{0,1\}$ with $Q=\{0,1\}$, shift rerandomization $\Prf(d) = c+d$ for $d \in \mathbb{Z}/3$, and the one-time-pad commitment. Let $c_A = (0,1)$ and $c_B = (0,2)$, both proper. Then for \emph{no} finite non-empty simulator randomness space $S$ and \emph{no} map $\Sm : R_v\times S \to (V \to \mathbb{Z}/3)$ does $\Sm$ perfectly simulate the opened coordinates of both $\mathcal{P}_{c_A}$ and $\mathcal{P}_{c_B}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Consider the opened view $t$ assigning colour $0$ to vertex $0$ and colour $1$ to vertex $1$. For $c_A$ the shifted colouring is $(d,1+d)$, equal to $(0,1)$ exactly when $d=0$, so $\Nreal^{A}(t)=1$ out of $|P|=3$. For $c_B$ the shifted colouring is $(d,2+d)$, never $(0,1)$, so $\Nreal^{B}(t)=0$. Perfect simulation of both would give $1\cdot|S| = \Nsim(t)\cdot 3$ and $0\cdot|S| = \Nsim(t)\cdot3$ with the \emph{same} $\Nsim(t)$ --- the simulator does not see the colouring and the query pattern is identical. The second forces $\Nsim(t)=0$, whence the first forces $|S|=0$, contradicting non-emptiness.
\end{proof}

A shift preserves the colour \emph{difference} along the challenged edge, so the opened pair reveals $c(e_2)-c(e_1)$, a genuine function of the witness. Sharp $2$-transitivity is exactly the property that kills every such invariant.

\section{Algorithmic content}

\textbf{A. Transcript-distribution comparator.} Enumerate $P\times R_c\times R_v$ and $S\times R_c\times R_v$, tally transcripts, compare the two rational distributions. Complexity $O((|P|+|S|)|R_c||R_v|\kappa)$, with $\kappa$ the cost of evaluating a transcript. Direct empirical test of Theorem~\ref{thm:main}.

\textbf{B. Fibre-count profiler.} For fixed $(T,c,o)$ compute $\fib(u,T,c,o)$ for all $u$ and verify constancy on classes of ``agrees on $T$''. Naively $O(|A|^{|I|}|R_c|)$; for the one-time pad $\fib \in \{0,1\}$ and is computed in $O(|I|)$. Tests Lemma~\ref{lem:fibcongr}.

\textbf{C. Local-view marginal checker.} For each $r$, tabulate $\Nreal(r,\cdot)$ and $\Nsim(r,\cdot)$ and verify Definition~\ref{def:sim}. Complexity $O((|P|+|S|)q)$ per verifier coin --- cheap, because only $q$ symbols matter.

\textbf{D. Sharp-transitivity certifier.} Given $A$, a subgroup $G \le \Sym(A)$ and a constraint (a set of admissible ordered tuples), build the bipartite incidence between $G$ and admissible tuples and test whether every tuple has exactly one preimage. Certifies the simulation resource in Theorem~\ref{thm:3col} and refutes it for the cyclic subgroup.

\section{Discussion and future directions}

\subsection{What the factorization buys}

Designing a perfectly zero-knowledge committed local-oracle protocol reduces to two independent engineering tasks: choose a commitment whose randomness space admits the re-labelling bijection of Definition~\ref{def:hiding}; and choose a rerandomization of the proof string making the $q$-symbol local view uniform on the admissible configurations. Neither task needs to know anything about the other, and the second is a finite group-theoretic question independent of the instance size.

\subsection{Future directions}

\paragraph{1. Locality-graded leakage: a $q$-query protocol leaks at most a $q$-local functional.} The fibrewise decomposition $\mathrm{count}(\tau) = |\{p : \Prf(p)|_Q = t\}|\cdot\fib(t)$ never mentions the coordinates outside $Q$, so any leakage must be a function of the $q$-marginal of the proof distribution --- a \emph{graded} statement interpolating between perfect zero knowledge ($q$-marginal witness-independent) and total leakage ($q = |I|$). The counting lemma already isolates the marginal; a quantitative statistical-distance bound needs only an $\ell^1$ version of the integral composition identity.

\paragraph{2. Group-theoretic characterization of alphabet rerandomization.} The simulation argument for $3$-colouring used nothing about colourings except that a group $G \le \Sym(A)$ acts sharply transitively on the admissible opened views; conversely, the cyclic counterexample shows that failure of that hypothesis kills simulation. Both halves are established here for $A = \mathbb{Z}/3$, $q=2$; the general conjecture is that a $q$-query constraint proof system over alphabet $A$ with prover rerandomization by $G$ is perfect honest-verifier zero knowledge \emph{iff} $G$ is transitive on each constraint's satisfying-assignment set --- an exact biconditional, provable with the same fibre machinery.

\paragraph{3. Zero knowledge is preserved by parallel repetition, exactly.} The product of two committed local-oracle protocols has transcript counts equal to the product of the counts, so the counting proof composes without any hybrid argument or slack. Exact parallel repetition for \emph{soundness} is already available in product form; pairing it with the transcript-count product would give an exact parallel-repetition theorem for zero knowledge as well.

\subsection{Limitations}

The framework is finite and information-theoretic. Perfect hiding in the sense of Definition~\ref{def:hiding} is incompatible with perfect binding, so compiled protocols are \emph{arguments} in any computational instantiation; the theorem isolates the information-theoretic core, with computational hiding replacing the bijection by an indistinguishability assumption and perfect equality by a negligible statistical distance. Only the \emph{honest}-verifier case is addressed. Finally, the simulator here commits and opens honestly --- natural for this compiler, but stronger than the general definition allows.

\section{Conclusion}

Zero knowledge for the ``local verifier plus commitment'' compiler is not one argument but two, meeting at right angles. Hiding governs the vertical direction --- the commitment randomness --- and guarantees that the number of coin sequences consistent with an observed (commitment, opening) pair depends on the message only through its opened part. Simulation governs the horizontal direction --- the prover's own randomness --- and guarantees that the opened part is itself witness-independent. Locality makes the horizontal direction small enough for the second guarantee to be achievable at all.

When both hold, they multiply, and the arithmetic is exact: real and simulated transcript distributions are equal, every event has the same probability under both, and every distinguisher, however powerful, has advantage exactly zero. For graph $3$-colouring the horizontal resource is the sharp $2$-transitivity of the symmetric group on three letters --- a fact about six permutations and six ordered pairs --- and we have shown both that this suffices and that weakening it to mere transitivity provably fails.

